    "description": "Food tourism routes connect ingredients, preparation, markets and meals within a particular landscape or urban setting. Rasht’s documented gastronomy heritage and Tabriz’s historic commercial fabric illustrate complementary ways to interpret food through place, without assuming that a cultural designation establishes an operating tour.",
    "tags": ["Food tourism", "Culinary heritage", "Rasht", "Tabriz", "Markets", "Regional travel"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T22:51:06Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T22:51:06Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Food Tourism Routes in Iran: Ingredients, Markets and Place | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iranian food routes through Rasht’s gastronomy heritage and Tabriz’s bazaar, with a clear distinction between cultural themes and verified tours."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "what-a-route-explains",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Following food beyond the plate",
        "content": "A food route becomes informative when it connects a meal to the ingredients, skills and places that make it possible. UNESCO’s account of Rasht links gastronomy with local resources and inherited cooking methods, while its account of Tabriz’s historic bazaar describes commerce as part of an integrated social and architectural system. These provide two different starting points: food within a productive landscape, and food within the wider life of a trading city. [s1][s2]\n\nThe route concepts below are editorial interpretations of those sources. They are not advertised itineraries, verified walking circuits or assertions that particular businesses currently receive visitors. Their purpose is to show what a coherent food journey could explain before transport, opening hours and individual hosts are confirmed.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "rasht-landscape-and-method",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Rasht: connect seasonal ingredients with cooking",
        "content": "Rasht, in the Caspian region, belongs to UNESCO’s Creative Cities Network in the field of gastronomy. UNESCO describes its food culture in relation to the fertile Sefid-Rud delta, local fish and seasonal produce. It also emphasizes inherited utensils and methods, including cooking in gamaj clay pots. [s1]\n\nA route built around this evidence would ask how a seasonal ingredient changes between its point of sale and the finished meal. A market encounter, an explanation of cookware and a discussion with a cook could form a connected sequence, provided access is arranged. The interpretive value lies in learning why a method suits an ingredient, rather than collecting unrelated dishes.\n\nUNESCO also describes training and urban-rural connections in Rasht’s gastronomy activity. Its profile includes aspirations for further initiatives; those aspirations should not be treated as proof that every proposed facility has opened. Network membership establishes a cultural-policy connection, not a guarantee of restaurant quality or visitor services. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "tabriz-market-as-system",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Tabriz: read a market as a working environment",
        "content": "Tabriz’s historic bazaar offers a different interpretive framework. UNESCO describes interconnected covered brick structures and enclosed spaces accommodating commerce alongside social, educational and religious functions. Its significance includes the relationship between specialized spaces, professions and the people using them. [s2]\n\nFor food interpretation, this suggests looking beyond a tasting stop to the commercial setting: how goods are displayed, how different activities share space, and how a working market accommodates visitors. This is a proposed line of inquiry, not a verified inventory of food stalls. The inspected heritage description does not establish a designated culinary trail or identify currently participating vendors.\n\nThe distinction matters because a bazaar is also a place of work. An informative visit should allow trading activity to remain the subject of observation rather than turning every shop into an assumed demonstration venue. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "route-evidence-and-conclusion",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From an interpretive theme to a usable itinerary",
        "content": "Before presenting a route as bookable, an editor should verify its named stops, host participation, language provision, duration, transport and cancellation conditions. These are proposed verification criteria, not claims about an existing Iranian route. A meal can illuminate regional heritage without making every ingredient local or every recipe exclusive to the destination.\n\nRasht and Tabriz demonstrate why food routes need different organizing questions. One can foreground seasonal resources and cooking knowledge; another can foreground exchange and the spaces supporting it. Keeping those questions explicit produces a more useful account of place than treating all destinations as interchangeable collections of restaurants. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "title": "Rasht",
